; An arbitrary Rust type Axiom holds as one opaque word.
;
; This is the mechanism that makes ecosystem leverage practical: a
; `sha2::Sha256`, a `reqwest::Client`, a `serde_json::Value` never has
; to be describable in Axiom's type system. Axiom holds the word, hands
; it back, and calls a registered destructor when it is done.
;
; The word gets `Foreign` and not `Int`, which is MM-FFI-5 requirement
; 1. The operational half of that distinction is requirement 2: a
; `Foreign` is excluded from MM-LIFE-2d's reference map, so no arena
; primitive and no release walk ever dereferences it. Declaring it `Int`
; would compile and would be a pointer the reference map is free to
; treat as a scalar today and something else tomorrow.
;
; Axiom is ARC with no finalizers, so `counterClose` runs when the
; program says so. There is no point at which the runtime could call it.
(import IO)
(import Fmt)
(import Demo)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (main)
  (let ((a (counterNew 41))
        (b (counterNew 100)))
    (let ((va (counterValue a))
          (vb (counterValue b)))
      {
        (counterClose a)
        (counterClose b)
        (if (&& (== va 41) (== vb 100))
            { (println "opaque handle: agree") 0 }
            { (println (fmtInt va)) (println (fmtInt vb)) 1 })
      })))
; expect 0
